037 What happened
038 On October 1, 2026, the Supreme Court agreed to decide whether the government must hold, while their removal cases are pending, people who are living in the United States and were never admitted. The order takes the case; it does not decide it. Two laws are in play. One, called here the detention statute, says that in the case of an applicant for admission, a person seeking admission "shall be detained" if an officer determines that person is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, except for a crewman, a stowaway, or a person an earlier provision of the detention statute already covers, and except that a person arriving by land from a contiguous territory may be returned there instead. The other, the bond statute, says the government "may release" a person on bond.
