043 For about thirty years, by the Second Circuit's account, people who entered without inspection and were not admitted or paroled, and who were not apprehended while entering or shortly afterward or held on a criminal or terrorism ground, could ask for bond. On July 10, 2025, a memorandum from the commissioner of Customs and Border Protection said the detention statute covers them instead. On September 5, 2025, the Board of Immigration Appeals said immigration judges lack authority to grant them bond. On September 26, 2025, officers arrested Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts; an immigration judge denied him a bond hearing, a district court ordered a bond hearing or release within ten days, and an immigration judge released him on bond after finding no danger and no flight risk. On April 28, 2026, the Second Circuit ruled the other way from the Board in Barbosa da Cunha's case. On October 1 the Supreme Court agreed to decide the question.
