074 Barbosa da Cunha
075 Barbosa da Cunha entered around 2005, lived in the United States for more than twenty years, applied for asylum in 2016, and held a work permit. He has never been arrested for or charged with a crime. If the government wins, people in his situation cannot be released on bond: the Second Circuit wrote that if the detention statute applies, he "must be detained during the removal process with no opportunity for release on bond". By September 10 the question had reached every numbered federal appeals court. Nine, including the Second and the Fourth, are the courts the Fourth Circuit said had held that the bond statute applies to noncitizens inside the country, like the people who filed the cases, and that they must be given a bond hearing. The Second Circuit's own holding covers people who entered without inspection and were not apprehended while entering or shortly afterward. Two, the Fifth and the Eighth, held that the detention statute applies to everyone who has not been admitted.
