100 Tens of thousands of immigrants
101 CBS News wrote that the Court would consider whether the administration can detain tens of thousands of immigrants during removal proceedings without bond hearings. No court or agency record gives a precise count of people held under the government's reading. The Fourth Circuit says the petitioners, the people who brought the cases, are among thousands of noncitizens swept up and detained without bond, and that district courts sided with the petitioners in over twenty thousand individual cases, which counts court cases, not people in detention.
102 19
103 11
104 Who the policy reaches
105 Customs and Border Protection's public notice describes mandatory detention for applicants for admission the Department of Homeland Security encounters for the first time after they fail to lawfully present for inspection at a port of entry. The July 10 memorandum says the first encounter counts "regardless of when or where that first encounter occurs". So the policy reaches a person arrested inside the country years after entering: Barbosa da Cunha was arrested driving to work in Norwood more than twenty years after he entered.
106 9
107 8
108 5
109 The practice
110 Where this sits
111 The 1997 Federal Register rule made people present without having been admitted or paroled eligible for bond.
112 The grant came 156 days after the Second Circuit's decision.
113 Date
114 Record
115 March 6, 1997
116 A Federal Register rule says people present without admission will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination.
117 July 8, 2025
118 The appeals opinion cites immigration enforcement guidance on detention of applicants for admission.
119 July 10, 2025
120 Customs and Border Protection says its commissioner issued detention guidance to every component.
121 Sept. 5, 2025
122 The Board of Immigration Appeals decides judges lack authority to grant bond in this situation.
123 Sept. 26, 2025
124 Officers arrested the man in this case while he was driving to work.
125 Apr. 28, 2026
126 The Second Circuit affirms the order requiring a bond hearing or release.
127 Sept. 10, 2026
128 The Fourth Circuit names the appeals courts that require a bond hearing and the two that do not.
129 Sept. 25, 2026
130 The Second Circuit denies rehearing by the full court.
131 Oct. 1, 2026
132 The Supreme Court grants review in this case.
133 On March 6, 1997, a Federal Register rule, effective April 1, said people present without having been admitted or paroled would be eligible for bond. The Second Circuit counts Executive Branch practice over thirty years and across five administrations, and it says the Justice Department understood the detention statute the same way when it issued that rule. On September 25, 2026, the full Second Circuit refused to rehear the case. Judge Steven J. Menashi dissented, arguing that the detention statute applies and that Barbosa da Cunha must be detained without a bond hearing. Judge Matthew A. Schwartz voted against rehearing while writing that he agreed with the dissent's analysis.
134 10
135 5
136 6
137 13
138 Despite being applicants for admission, aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled (formerly referred to as aliens who entered without inspection) will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination.
139 10
140 record
141 On April 28, 2026, the Second Circuit affirmed the district court. It held that detention of a person in the United States after entering without inspection and admission, who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter, follows the bond statute, not mandatory detention. Judge Cabranes agreed and filed a separate opinion. The appeal was argued April 6.
142 5
143 record
144 In July 2025 the government changed its practice. The opinion cites immigration enforcement guidance dated July 8, 2025. The petition names that guidance as Todd M. Lyons, Acting Director of Immigration and Customs Enforcement, and says applicants for admission may not be released from custody except by parole. Divided panels in the Fifth and Eighth Circuits agreed with the government.
145 5
146 12
147 5
148 record
149 On September 25, 2026, the Second Circuit denied rehearing by the full court. Judges Bianco and Nathan, joined by four others, concurred in that denial. Judge Menashi dissented.
150 6
151 record
152 On September 5, 2025, the Board of Immigration Appeals held that immigration judges lack authority to hear bond requests, or to grant bond, for people present in the United States without admission.
153 7
154 record
155 The signed memorandum, a one-page scan, is addressed from Commissioner Rodney S. Scott. It says the department's position is that applicants for admission are subject to mandatory detention and may not be released except by parole.
156 8
157 record
158 Open questions
159 What we do not know yet
160 No argument date has been set. The Court took one question, what the detention statute means. Whether holding someone without a bond hearing violates the Constitution's guarantee of due process is not part of it; the government's petition says a second case, Lopez-Campos from the Sixth Circuit, raises that question as well. The Court had not granted or denied review in that case or in a third, Buenrostro-Mendez from the Fifth Circuit, as of October 3. A motion to extend the time to respond was granted in Lopez-Campos, and Buenrostro-Mendez was rescheduled. Both were sent to the same September 28 conference.
161 3
162 2
163 12
164 14
165 15
166 Customs and Border Protection's public notice says Commissioner Rodney S. Scott issued guidance on July 10, 2025, and that applicants for admission encountering the department for the first time after failing to lawfully present for inspection at a port of entry are subject to mandatory detention.
167 9
168 record
169 The question, the statutes, and one count in the coverage
170 The question presented reads: whether the cited detention provision mandates detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like the respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted.
171 2
172 The 2024 compilation of the detention section says that if the examining officer determines an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained for a removal proceeding. The bond section says the government may release an arrested alien on bond of at least $1,500.
173 16
174 17
175 70 days from the July 23 petition to the October 1 grant. 450 days from the July 8 guidance date in the opinion to the grant. 448 days from the July 10 Customs guidance to the grant. 391 days from the September 5 Board decision to the grant. Counted from those dates.
176 CBS News wrote that the Court would consider whether the administration can detain tens of thousands of immigrants without bond hearings. That count is not in the order, the docket, the appeals opinion, the Board decision, or the Customs memorandum.
177 19
178 The Associated Press wrote that the Court agreed on Thursday to review a policy denying bond hearings. The Hill wrote that the Supreme Court announced Thursday it will decide the legality of President Trump's mandatory detention policy. Both match the October 1 grant.
