209-5 A noncitizen detained under this subsection can be released only through a mechanism known as “parole” for “urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit,” pursuant to 8 U.S.C. § 1182(d)(5)(A). Parole creates its own legal fiction. If a noncitizen is paroled, he is not considered to have been “admitted” into the United States, and he is treated “as if [he was] stopped at the border.” Dep’t of Homeland Sec. v. Thuraissigiam, 591 U.S. 103, 139 (2020) (quoting Mezei, 345 U.S. at 215).
