259:Third, and significantly, even if Congress did intend to create a legal fiction by “deeming” the Petitioners to be “applicants for admission,” it did not “deem” them to be “seeking admission,” “applying for admission,” or to have “submitted an application for admission.” Each of these phrases appears elsewhere in the statute. Standing alone and unmodified, “applicants for admission” plainly applies to the Petitioners because they are present in the United States without inspection and authorization. Section 1225(b)(2)(A), however, modifies the phrase “applicant for admission” by adding the additional phrase, “seeking admission.” That modification is important, and this court’s precedent in Hedin v. Thompson illustrates why. Hedin concerned the proper construction of the Health Professions Education Partnerships Act. 355 F.3d at 748. One section of that statute provided that “[a]ctive service” in the Commissioned Corps of the United States Public Health Service “shall be deemed to be active military service in the Armed Forces of the United States for purposes of all laws related to discrimination on the basis of race, color, sex, ethnicity, age, religion, and disability.” Id. (quoting 42 U.S.C. § 213(f)). This deeming provision created a legal fiction whereby someone who was not serving in the military was nonetheless “deemed to be” serving in the military for the narrow purpose articulated by Congress. Id. at 748–49. Where the phrase “active service” stood alone and unmodified, Hedin treated it as a deeming provision that included individuals serving in the Public Health Service. Id. at 749. Here too, where “applicants for admission” is unmodified, the phrase includes noncitizens like the Petitioners who are present without inspection and admission. See, e.g., 8 U.S.C. § 1229a.
