348:“certain aspects” of this differential treatment. H.R. Rep. No. 104-469, pt. 1, at 225 (emphasis added). With respect to the detention statutes specifically, the legislative history indicates that Congress intended to maintain the prior distinctions. The legislative history report cited by the Government emphasizes that Section 1226 “restates” the pre-IIRIRA Section 1252(a)(1) “regarding the authority of the Attorney General to arrest, detain, and release on bond [a noncitizen] who is not lawfully in the United States.” H.R. Rep. No. 104-469, pt. 1, at 229. The predecessor statute to Section 1226 provided for a bond hearing for noncitizens present in the United States without inspection and authorization. 8 U.S.C. § 1252(a)(1) (1995). The legislative history makes no mention of narrowing bond eligibility for such noncitizens. Moreover, the legislative changes enacted in IIRIRA indicate that Congress intended to broaden the scope of the bond eligibility provisions to continue to apply to the same noncitizens it had applied to historically. “When Congress amends legislation, [we] presume it intends the change to have real and substantial effect.” Van Buren v. United States, 593 U.S. 374, 393 (2021) (quoting Ross v. Blake, 578 U.S. 632, 641–42 (2016)). The predecessor statute to Section 1226 used the phrase “[p]ending a determination of deportability” consistent with the prior statute’s dual-track framework for deportation and exclusionary proceedings. See 8 U.S.C. § 1252(a)(1) (1995). In IIRIRA, Congress explicitly removed the word “deportability,” replacing it with the word, “removed.” 8 U.S.C. § 1226(a). The use of “removed” broadens the section to encompass IIRIRA’s newly defined categories of both inadmissible and deportable noncitizens. With this
349-
350---- page 44 ---
351-44
352-
353:change, Congress ensured that this same procedure continues to apply to noncitizens like the Petitioners. The statutory and legislative history of IIRIRA also reflects Congress’s purpose in enacting Section 1225(b). The House Report on IIRIRA describes Section 1225(b) as “establish[ing] new procedures for the inspection and in some cases removal of aliens arriving in the United States.” H.R. Rep. No. 104-828, at 209 (1996). Section 1225(b) also traces its roots to prior statutory provisions. The Immigration Act of 1893 states, “it shall be the duty of every inspector of arriving alien immigrants to detain for a special inquiry . . . every person who may not appear to him to be clearly and beyond doubt entitled to admission.” 27 Stat. 569, 570. The language of this predecessor statute echoes in the formulation of Section 1225(b)(2)(A): “[I]f the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained for a [removal] proceeding[.]” Seeking admission is not surplusage. Rather, it is an essential phrase that limits the application of the subsection to noncitizens at the border. Though the Government’s argument rests heavily on legislative history, we disagree that the legislative history tips in the Government’s favor. Rather, the statutory and legislative history both demonstrate that Congress intended for “seeking admission” to have a meaning different from and in addition to “applicant for admission.” Seeking admission is a modifier indicating an additional element that must be met, not a characteristic imputed to every applicant for admission.
